<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Keychain access group.

	  Context, stated precisely: this project previously had NO
	  CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS and no entitlements file, and in that state
	  every SecItem call failed with -34018 (errSecMissingEntitlement).
	  react-native-keychain still linked and registered, which is what made
	  it look installed — the JS module imported, the native methods were
	  exposed, and every read and write failed. Sessions were memory-only
	  on device: sign-in worked, relaunch did not, and sign-out could not
	  erase a token it had never stored.

	  -34018 does NOT universally mean "no entitlement". It means the app
	  is not permitted to use the access group it attempted; a mismatched
	  group or a profile that does not grant the group produces the same
	  code. And `keychain-access-groups` is not strictly required for an
	  app's own private items — with no explicit accessGroup the default
	  group derives from the signed `application-identifier`. This file
	  declares the group explicitly so the grant is intentional and
	  reviewable rather than implicit in whatever profile happens to sign
	  the build.

	  `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier)` resolves at build time
	  to team prefix + bundle id, so it needs no edit when either changes.
	  NOTE: the bundle id is still the React Native placeholder
	  (org.reactjs.native.example.keystone-app). A Debug device build
	  therefore validates the PLACEHOLDER identity and a development
	  profile — not the distribution identity. The release/CI check must
	  assert the real values on the built product.
	-->
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier)</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
